@*
/// <summary>
/// Patient introduction and onboarding view for the DLDA assessment.
/// The layout is deliberately kept simple, centralized, and distraction-free to minimize cognitive load 
/// for psychiatric patients, ensuring they understand the purpose of the assessment 
/// and feel secure before proceeding to the actual questionnaire.
/// </summary>
*@
<div class="container mt-5 text-center">
    <h1 class="display-4">DLDA</h1>
    <h4 class="mb-4">Hur jag upplever min funktion i vardagen</h4>

    <img src="~/Images/dlda_intro.png" class="img-fluid mb-3" style="max-width: 280px;" />

    <h5 class="mt-4">Vad handlar bedömningen om?</h5>

    @* 
    // This visually distinct frame highlights the core reassurance: that skipping questions is allowed. 
    // Emphasizing this reduces patient performance anxiety and lowers the barrier to participation, 
    // which is a critical clinical requirement for this specific psychiatric assessment tool. 
    *@
    <div style="border: 2px solid #198754; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 12px; max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto; background-color: #f8f9fa;">
        <p class="lead mb-0">
            Frågorna handlar om din vardag. När du svarat kommer personalen att kunna prata med dig om hur stödet kan planeras på bästa sätt.<br />
            Känn dig fri att hoppa över frågor som känns svåra att svara på.
        </p>
    </div>

    @* 
    // We utilize the browser's native history API for navigation rather than a hardcoded server-side route.
    // This makes the view context-independent, returning the patient exactly to their previous state 
    // (e.g., a specific dashboard tab or an overview list) without breaking the expected back-navigation flow.
    *@
    <div class="d-flex justify-content-center mt-5" style="max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto;">
        <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href="javascript:history.back()">
            🔙 Tillbaka
        </a>
    </div>
</div>